Subject: [PATCH] drivers: i2c: stm32_v2: contained backport of the upstream ISR rework

Baseline: zephyr v3.6.0 (468eb56cf242eedba62006ee758700ee6148763f)
Applies to: drivers/i2c/i2c_ll_stm32_v2.c (interrupt mode only) and
drivers/i2c/i2c_ll_stm32.h. Supersedes the local patch 0004, which is
deleted in the same commit.

Backported from upstream Zephyr, which is the specification here:

  34b756f803796eefef81c411fa7a1bb5da6a9bcf
      drivers: i2c_stm32_v2: Rework interrupt handler          (v4.3)
  69bfd434e1d... drivers: i2c_stm32_v2: fix assert in i2c event isr

This is NOT a cherry-pick and does not claim to be byte-identical to
either. Upstream landed them on a driver that by then had DMA support,
cache maintenance, a per-message timeout, split public headers and a
renamed API; both refuse to apply to v3.6.0. What is taken is the state
machine and the synchronising read, transcribed onto this driver, with the
DMA and cache paths left out because this integration does not build them
and with this integration's error codes kept (see below).

WHY, measured on DS20001. Two runs of an L7 diagnostic image wedged the
board at interrupt level. The terminal state, sampled once a second from a
timer interrupt while every thread starved, was RXNE asserted with the
driver's byte count already 0, RXIE still enabled, and TC, TCR, STOPF and
NACKF all clear -- an entry the old handler can neither consume nor mask,
re-entered about 890,000 times a second against a healthy 35,500.

The old handler has two defects that lead there, and the second one is why
the first fix was not enough:

  - the last segment of a transfer split at 255 bytes kept RELOAD set, so
    it ended in TCR instead of TC. Patch 0004 addressed only this. Time to
    failure went from 3 min 40 s to 12 min 6 s, and the routine background
    of "RXNE with len 0" went from about one per read to three in ten
    minutes -- but the board still wedged.
  - the buffer is advanced whenever current.len != 0, WITHOUT checking that
    the entry carried RXNE or TXIS. The second run caught exactly that: one
    entry carrying only TCR|BUSY, counted as a byte received, at the same
    millisecond as the wedge. From then on software was one byte ahead of
    the hardware, which is how RXNE arrives with len already 0.

The upstream rework fixes both by construction: one status read per entry,
exactly one mutually exclusive event handled per entry with NACK and STOP
taken first because they end the transfer whatever else is set, the buffer
advanced ONLY in the RXNE and TXIS branches one byte at a time, TCR as its own branch that reprograms NBYTES and clears
RELOAD for a final segment, TC generating the STOP, and segmentation of
messages over 255 bytes done inside the handler instead of by the calling
thread re-entering a half-configured peripheral.

ONE CORRECTION TO UPSTREAM, not a transcription: on timeout this driver
disables the transfer interrupts with interrupts locked and then empties
the completion semaphore. Upstream only disables the peripheral, and the
v3.6 code it replaces did the equivalent through master_mode_end and an
explicit give/take pair. Without it, a completion that races the 500 ms
timeout leaves a token behind and the NEXT transfer takes it and returns
success before the hardware has done anything.

KEPT FROM THIS INTEGRATION, deliberately diverging from upstream:

  - the error classification of patch 0001. Upstream returns -EIO for every
    failure; the ToF chain enumeration needs a clean NACK (-ENXIO, nobody
    at this address) told apart from a broken transport (-EIO) and from a
    timeout (-ETIMEDOUT). The priority is unchanged: timeout, then bus
    error or arbitration loss, then NACK.
  - the fixed STM32_I2C_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT_MSEC rather than upstream's
    per-message timeout. With segmentation now inside the handler a single
    call covers a whole message, and 500 ms covers the largest this
    integration issues with room to spare.
  - the forensics hook on entry to the event handler, which patch 0003
    declares and this patch places, because this patch owns the function.

NOT ADDRESSED HERE, and still required before this can be called done: an
I2C bus recovery at boot before commissioning. A watchdog reset does not
restore the sensors -- they keep half-finished transaction state across it,
which is why E3 could not re-commission after either hang. No ISR fix
reaches that.

